PRD — Live Cricket Match Simulation & Scoring Engine
Status: Draft
Version: 1.0
Type: Backend / Machine Coding Project

1. Objective
Build an in-memory cricket match simulation engine that plays a limited-overs match ball by ball and produces a final scorecard.

The system should simulate the match realistically enough to demonstrate correct cricket scoring, while keeping the implementation simple and easy to extend.

2. Users
Simulator/User: Starts a match and views the final result.
Stats Consumer: Uses batting and bowling statistics.
Engineering Reviewer: Reviews the design, correctness, and testability.
3. Scope
The first version will support:

T20 and ODI matches
Two teams with 11 players each
Toss and innings management
Ball-by-ball simulation
Runs: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6
Wickets: Bowled, Caught and Run-out
Wides and No-balls
Striker/non-striker rotation
Bowler over limits
Second-innings chase
Final batting and bowling scorecards
Deterministic results for automated testing
4. Match Flow
Create Match
     ↓
Validate Teams
     ↓
Toss
     ↓
First Innings
     ↓
Calculate Target
     ↓
Second Innings
     ↓
Check Win / All-Out / Overs
     ↓
Generate Result & Scorecard
The first innings ends when the team is all out or completes its allotted overs.

The second innings additionally ends immediately when the chasing team reaches the target.

5. Scoring Rules
Every delivery must update the appropriate statistics.

For example:

A normal 4 gives 4 runs to the batter and team.
A wide gives an extra run to the team and bowler but not the batter.
A no-ball does not count as a legal delivery.
A no-ball with 4 runs off the bat gives 5 team runs but 4 batter runs.
An over consists of six legal deliveries, so wides and no-balls do not count toward the six.
1 and 3 runs normally change the striker.
Striker and non-striker swap at the end of every completed over.
A run-out may dismiss either the striker or non-striker.
6. Scorecard
Batting
The scorecard will show:

Player
Runs
Balls faced
4s
6s
Dismissal
Bowling
The scorecard will show:

Bowler
Overs
Runs conceded
Wickets
7. Match Rules
Format	Overs	Max Overs/Bowler
T20	20	4
ODI	50	10
The format-specific rules should be configurable so that another limited-overs format can be added later without changing the main match flow.

8. Important Edge Cases
The system must correctly handle:

All-out before the allotted overs
Successful chase before the allotted overs
Tie
Wides and no-balls
Run-out of the non-striker
Bowler reaching the maximum over limit
More than six physical deliveries in an over because of wides/no-balls
No available eligible bowler
Last available batter being dismissed
9. Testing & Reliability
The simulator currently uses random outcomes, but randomness must be injectable/seedable so automated tests can reproduce the same match.

